Batch-adaptive $\tau$ yields no improvement over fixed $\tau$ (single-seed exploratory); both hyperparameters have default values that perform well without per-model tuning.


\subsection{Constraint Regime Analysis}
\label{sec:regime}

Over 25{,}991 training micro-batches (seed\,42, $\tau{=}0.8$; qualitatively similar across $\tau \in [0.7, 0.8]$), only ${\sim}2\%$ are clean ($\aeff(0) \geq \tau$); among the rest, 38.2\% are $\aeff$-binding and 61.8\% ESS-binding (33.4\% $\aeff$-binding at $\rho{=}0.6$; Appendix~\ref{app:regime}).
In $\aeff$-binding batches, $\bstar$ is 4.9$\times$ lower than the ESS-only upper bound (mean 1.19 vs.\ 5.88); the per-batch distribution spans mean 1.43 (std 1.93, range $[0, 20]$) and is stable across seeds (CV ${<}2\%$).

